{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789920561,"nonce":"k461+/P/EEv3XaBBc0XVByOa/Bc=","title":"LLVM: a function's address as a value was refused","message":"HirCallable::FuncRef, the SSA builder's way of taking a function's address (for a closure record's code pointer), was an error in the LLVM backend, so every function creating a closure failed to compile at the LLVM tier and stayed on Cranelift; the failure was silent but for ZYNTAX_OSR_TRACE. Cranelift answers it with the function's address or its cell's entry. LLVM now does the same: the module's own function's address, else the entry read from the cross-tier cell, which the direct-callee scan already provides.","files":null}]}